From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Elias Bakken <elias@iagent.no>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 23:55:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] sunxi: arm64: make bootm_size cover the layout it ships with

Booting Reflash off USB dies just before the kernel is entered:

    ## Loading init Ramdisk from Legacy Image at 4ff00000 ...
       Data Size:    87794438 Bytes = 83.7 MiB
       Verifying Checksum ... OK
    ## Flattened Device Tree blob at 4fa00000
       Booting using the fdt blob at 0x4fa00000
    ramdisk - allocation error

boot_ramdisk_high() allocates the relocated initrd out of
[bootm_low, bootm_low + bootm_size), which is 0x40000000..0x4a000000 with
the shipped bootm_size of 160MB. On this board that window holds, in
order:

    0x40080000..0x428d0000   kernel (loaded 39.7MB, moved to 0x40200000)
    0x45000000..0x45000eed   armbianEnv.txt, then the DT fixup script
    0x46000000..0x4600ae14   the eMMC DTB, read to identify the board

leaving a 40MB hole and a 67MB hole. The initrd is 83.7MB, so it fits in
neither and the allocation fails.

Those scratch reservations are new in effect rather than in kind: the
boot script has always used load_addr=0x45000000, but LMB is a persistent
global map now and fs_read_lmb_check() reserves every load destination
for the rest of the session. Under v2024.01 the map was rebuilt per
bootm and the scratch loads were invisible by the time booti ran, which
is why the same image and the same script boot there.

Even unfragmented the window was marginal - 124MB of kernel plus initrd
in 160MB - and it contradicts the layout immediately below it, which puts
RAMDISK_ADDR_R at 255MB and reserves "256MB for really big kernels".
Widen it to the 512MB the same comment says these boards have. LMB never
hands out memory that is absent or already reserved, so on a smaller
board this just stops being the binding constraint rather than becoming
a way to overrun RAM.

Signed-off-by: Elias Bakken <elias@iagent.no>
---
--- a/include/configs/sunxi-common.h
+++ b/include/configs/sunxi-common.h
@@ -76,8 +76,27 @@
  * There is no compression for arm64 kernels (yet), so leave some space
  * for really big kernels, say 256MB for now.
  * Scripts, PXE and DTBs should go afterwards, leaving the rest for the initrd.
+ *
+ * bootm_size has to span that whole layout, not a fraction of it. It bounds
+ * where bootm may relocate the kernel, the FDT and above all the initrd, and
+ * the load addresses below already run to RAMDISK_ADDR_R at 255MB - so a 160MB
+ * window could never hold what this layout loads. Two things then go wrong on a
+ * distro boot:
+ *
+ *  - Size alone. A 40MB kernel plus an 84MB initrd is 124MB of the 160, and
+ *    that is before anything else is placed.
+ *  - Fragmentation. LMB is a persistent global map now, and every "load"
+ *    reserves its destination for the rest of the session (fs_read_lmb_check).
+ *    Armbian's boot script uses load_addr=0x45000000 as scratch, so by the time
+ *    booti runs, the window is cut into 40MB and 67MB pieces with the initrd
+ *    fitting in neither, and boot_ramdisk_high() fails with
+ *    "ramdisk - allocation error".
+ *
+ * 512MB is the whole of the smallest arm64 sunxi board's DRAM, which is the
+ * right bound: LMB will not hand out memory that is absent or already spoken
+ * for, so on a larger board this simply stops being the binding constraint.
  */
-#define BOOTM_SIZE        __stringify(0xa000000)
+#define BOOTM_SIZE        __stringify(0x20000000)
 #define KERNEL_ADDR_R     __stringify(SDRAM_OFFSET(0080000))
 #define KERNEL_COMP_ADDR_R __stringify(SDRAM_OFFSET(4000000))
 #define KERNEL_COMP_SIZE  __stringify(0xb000000)
-- 
2.43.0

